WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.420
In our writing app,

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.610
published stories go to a public feed,

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.490
and drafts stay private.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.630
You ask your coding agent to

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:09.850
add trending topics to that feed.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.240
It takes a shortcut:

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.760
it wires the feed straight to the recommender.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.970
But your architecture keeps the public side apart

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.040
from anything that works with private drafts,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.790
and the recommender does.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.690
The agent didn't know.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.240
That boundary is a rule in your Shape model.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.490
The agent records its new connection in the model,

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.650
as the check requires,

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:29.850
and the Shape check on the

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.750
pull request stops the merge.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.670
The check names each connection on the path,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.980
so you can see which one crosses the line.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.900
So the agent goes the way the architecture allows:

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.650
trending topics come from a service

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.180
that only uses published stories.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:45.940
The check passes.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.410
Shape follows every connection you write down, however indirect.
